  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating and comparing the skewness of the two lotteries—specifically, which one concentrates more probability on favorable (less negative) outcomes. This involves a holistic assessment of the distribution of probabilities across outcomes. The individual, however, focuses narrowly on the worst outcomes under the same 25% probability, comparing -6000 in A versus -4000 in B, and judges A negatively due to the higher loss. There is no indication that the individual considered the 75% chance of 0 in A versus the 50% in B, nor any comparison of how probability mass is distributed across the spectrum of outcomes—key to skewness. The justification is based on loss aversion or minimizing maximum loss at given probabilities, not on distributional asymmetry. Therefore, the information considered by the individual does not align with the information required by the decision reason, and the justification rule (avoid high losses at same probability) differs from the reason’s rule (prefer more left-skewed distribution).",
